#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[Notes on the literature]
<notes:applications>

#notes-on(<sec:weil-formula>) Here we are mainly presenting well-known results.
Formula @eq:weil-formula of @ss:weil-representation[subsection] was proved by
Weil @bib:Weil1935 for arbitrary relatively compact polyhedra (and not only for
special ones). There is a presentation of it in the books of Vladimirov
@bib:Vladimirov1964, Shabat @bib:Shabat1976, and Aizenberg and Yuzhakov
@bib:Aizenberg1979b. On the history of this formula and its connection with
other questions of complex analysis see the survey of Khenkin @bib:Khenkin1985
and also the article of Gleason @bib:Gleason1963. The modification of Weil's
formula given in @ss:weil-modification[subsection] is close in principle to a
formula of Vitushkin (see @bib:Khenkin1985). The local variant of Hilbert's
theorem on roots stated in @ss:local-nullstellensatz[subsection] is a special
case of an assertion from the local theory of analytic sets (see
@bib:Gunning1965, @bib:Herve1966). The existence of the expansion
@eq:wpt-expansion in the Weierstrass preparation theorem was proved by Houzel
@bib:Houzel1961; a direct proof of this expansion was given in an article of
Arnolʹd @bib:Arnold1968. The uniqueness of the expansion @eq:wpt-expansion was
noted by Palamodov in @bib:Palamodov1967a, where he made essential use of the
results of the book @bib:Palamodov1967b. More general theorems on division with
remainder are also in Palamodov's book @bib:Palamodov1967b. The theorem on the
separation of a pseudopolynomial mapping was not known earlier to the author.

#notes-on(<sec:weakly-holomorphic>) #source(171)General facts about weakly
holomorphic functions were obtained by Oka, Cartan, Remmert, Spallek, and
others. In @sec:weakly-holomorphic weakly holomorphic functions on complete
intersections are studied more thoroughly. The main results were obtained by
Tsikh; these were based on the results and ideas of the following earlier works:
on a criterion for the extendability of weakly holomorphic functions---Chirka
@bib:Harvey1977, Schwartz @bib:Schwartz1953, and Khenkin @bib:Khenkin1972, and
on integral representations on analytic sets---Khenkin @bib:Khenkin1972, Stout
@bib:Stout1975, Henkin and Leiterer @bib:Henkin1984, and Palm @bib:Palm1976. The
results of Corollaries~@cor:smooth-extension and~@cor:normality of
@ss:weakly-extension[subsection] are due to Spallek @bib:Spallek1965 and
Abhyankar @bib:Abhyankar1960 respectively. The example of incomplete
intersection in @ss:weakly-extension[subsection] was taken from the book of
Shafarevich @bib:Shafarevich1972, where it was reduced to the subject of a set
with a "fine" singularity that is not normal.

#notes-on(<sec:logarithmic-residue>) The theorem of
@ss:complete-intersection-residue[subsection] is due to Coleff and Herrera
@bib:Coleff1978, and the theorem of @ss:overdetermined[subsection] is due to
Tsikh.

#notes-on(<sec:multiplicity>) The main results of subsections
@ss:resultant-multiplicity[n] and @ss:initial-terms[n] were obtained by Tsikh
@bib:Tsikh1976, @bib:Yuzhakov1978. The coincidence of the geometric and
algebraic multiplicities was proved by Palamodov @bib:Palamodov1967a; for
another proof see @bib:Arnold1982[§5].

#notes-on(<sec:polynomial-ideals>) Noether's theorem was proved by Noether in
$PP_2$. The possibility of proving it by means of local duality and Serre
duality was mentioned by Griffiths and Harris @bib:Griffiths1978a. The proof of
this theorem in $PP_n$ (with just the use of local duality) was taken from
Tsikh's article @bib:Tsikh1984a. The theorem in
@ss:noether-conditions[subsection] was proved in the case $n = 2$ by Bertini;
for arbitrary $n$ it was obtained by Tsikh @bib:Tsikh1984a, @bib:Tsikh1985a.
